From: Kyvenza <support@kyvenza.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ui/vnc: do not back off the refresh timer while a job is in flight

vnc_refresh() halves the refresh interval when the last pass produced
rectangles and otherwise backs off by VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_INC (50 ms) up
to VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MAX.  The "otherwise" branch is also taken when the
screen *is* dirty but vnc_update_client() could not queue a job because the
worker thread is still busy encoding the previous update (job_update !=
VNC_STATE_UPDATE_NONE) or the output queue is above the throttle threshold.

On a 2560x1600 guest with an interactive client this turns every frame
that takes longer than one refresh interval to encode into a 50 ms penalty,
followed by a halving recovery ramp.  Measured with a Tight client on
Apple silicon: the interval oscillates between 30 ms and ~80-130 ms and the
client sees 12-17 fps while the worker is only 28% busy.

Treat "dirty but the client is still consuming the previous update" as
neither progress nor idleness: hold the interval where it is.  Real idleness
(no dirty pixels) still backs off exactly as before, so the CPU-saving
behaviour for idle guests is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Kyvenza <support@kyvenza.com>
---

--- a/ui/vnc.c
+++ b/ui/vnc.c
@@ -3250,6 +3250,7 @@
     VncDisplay *vd = container_of(dcl, VncDisplay, dcl);
     VncState *vs, *vn;
     int has_dirty, rects = 0;
+    bool busy = false;
 
     if (QTAILQ_EMPTY(&vd->clients)) {
         qemu_console_listener_set_refresh(&vd->dcl, VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MAX);
@@ -3267,7 +3268,18 @@
     vnc_unlock_display(vd);
 
     QTAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE(vs, &vd->clients, next, vn) {
-        rects += vnc_update_client(vs, has_dirty);
+        /* Read before the call: vs may be free()ed by vnc_update_client(). */
+        bool wants_update = vs->update != VNC_STATE_UPDATE_NONE;
+        int n = vnc_update_client(vs, has_dirty);
+        rects += n;
+        /*
+         * A client that has asked for an update but got nothing this pass
+         * is still consuming the previous one: the worker is encoding it or
+         * the socket has not drained yet.  That is not idleness.
+         */
+        if (n == 0 && has_dirty && wants_update) {
+            busy = true;
+        }
         /* vs might be free()ed here */
     }
 
@@ -3276,6 +3288,12 @@
         if (vd->dcl.update_interval < VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_BASE) {
             vd->dcl.update_interval = VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_BASE;
         }
+    } else if (has_dirty && busy) {
+        /*
+         * Dirty, but the client is still busy with the last update.  Hold
+         * the interval: backing off here only delays the next frame once
+         * the client is ready, and halving back takes several passes.
+         */
     } else {
         vd->dcl.update_interval += VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_INC;
         if (vd->dcl.update_interval > VNC_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MAX) {
